Relapsing Polychondritis (Mild Auricular)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Red, painful, swollen ear (sparing the earlobe), self-limiting flares

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-040
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of a severely painful, swollen, and red ear requiring immediate exclusion of acute mastoiditis or severe bacterial perichondritis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute, intense inflammation of the external ear. Immediate clinical review was mandatory to rule out acute mastoiditis, severe bacterial perichondritis, or malignant otitis externa, which would require immediate emergency department transfer and intravenous antibiotics prior to the next in-hours period to prevent cartilage necrosis or intracranial spread.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My right ear is burning hot, completely swollen, and the pain is so intense I can't even rest my head on a pillow. I think I have a severe infection.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports isolated episodic ear cartilage inflammation historically. Tonight, the patient experienced a sudden, severe flare of right ear pain and swelling evolving over 6 hours. The upper portion of the ear is exquisitely tender, preventing the patient from sleeping on that side. The patient is highly distressed by the rapid swelling and fears permanent deformity (cauliflower ear) or a spreading brain infection. Denies fever, recent ear piercing, trauma, purulent discharge from the canal, or hearing loss.
Functional Status: Independent, but severely sleep-deprived due to pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Low risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 92 | Temp: 37.0 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Alert, in obvious acute discomfort, holding head tilted away from the affected side.
Physical Exam: Head and neck exam reveals a red, painful, swollen ear. Crucially, the inflammation strictly involves the cartilaginous upper pinna, entirely sparing the earlobe (which lacks cartilage). The affected area is violently erythematous, warm, and exquisitely tender to light palpation. The external auditory canal is clear without purulence, and the tympanic membrane is normal. The mastoid process is completely non-tender to deep percussion. Self-limiting flares noted in history. No facial nerve palsy.
Environmental Observations: Safe environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient has taken no current immunosuppressants or NSAIDs tonight.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute flare of Relapsing Polychondritis (Mild Auricular) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Mastoiditis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Completely normal, non-tender mastoid process, normal tympanic membrane, and absence of outward displacement of the ear.

Bacterial Perichondritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Sparing of the earlobe (classic for polychondritis), afebrile status, and lack of recent penetrating trauma (e.g., high ear piercing).

Erysipelas/Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The sharp demarcation of inflammation stopping exactly where the cartilage ends (sparing the lobule), whereas cellulitis typically involves the entire skin surface uniformly.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acutely red, swollen, and painful ear, a high-risk presentation mimicking severe bacterial perichondritis or mastoiditis. Triage correctly prioritized a physical examination to prevent cartilage necrosis or deep tissue infection. Bedside evaluation revealed the classic pathognomonic sign of relapsing polychondritis—severe inflammation strictly confined to the cartilaginous structures, distinctly sparing the earlobe. The mastoid and ear canal were entirely clear. This rapid, accurate diagnosis successfully averted an emergency department admission for unnecessary intravenous antibiotics and surgical drainage, entirely satisfying the criteria for urgent after-hours intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered high-dose oral NSAID (e.g., indomethacin or ibuprofen) immediately. Provided a cold compress for acute symptomatic relief.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply an ice pack wrapped in a soft cloth to the inflamed cartilage for 15 minutes every two hours, and sleep on your opposite side tonight. Chilling the tissue directly constricts the overactive blood vessels, immediately halting the intense burning pain and preventing further swelling while the anti-inflammatory medication takes effect.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Urgent GP or rheumatology review within 24-48 hours to assess response and consider oral corticosteroids if the flare persists.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if the redness spreads down onto your neck or face, if you develop a severe, deep throbbing pain behind the ear (over the mastoid bone), or if you develop a high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive regarding the exact autoimmune trigger; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Your immune system is mistakenly attacking the cartilage in your ear. It looks alarming but responds very well to strong anti-inflammatory medications and will not spread to your brain.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical review if you notice thick yellow pus draining from inside the ear canal, or if your hearing suddenly drops in that ear.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The redness and pain are from severe immune inflammation, not a bacterial infection. The fact that your earlobe is perfectly normal proves this, as the lobe has no cartilage to attack.
Actionable Steps: Do not rub, massage, or apply heat to the ear. Use only cold compresses. You must follow up with your doctor promptly to ensure the cartilage does not become permanently damaged.

